WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.357 --> 00:00:02.197
- It's great to go to film school,

00:00:02.197 --> 00:00:05.060
and you can learn a lot
of intellectual knowledge,

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.460
but, learn by doing.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:11.117
If you have a golf ball
sized consciousness,

00:00:11.117 --> 00:00:12.548
when you read a book,

00:00:12.548 --> 00:00:15.648
you'll have a golf ball
sized understanding.

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:17.301
Meditate regularly.

00:00:17.301 --> 00:00:21.049
And then, the second thing,
I do, is drink coffee.

00:00:21.049 --> 00:00:23.752
- He's an American director,
producer, screenwriter,

00:00:23.752 --> 00:00:25.155
and photographer.

00:00:25.155 --> 00:00:27.420
His films have been
known to disturb, offend,

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.414
and mystify audiences.

00:00:29.414 --> 00:00:32.194
He was described by the
Guardian as the most important

00:00:32.194 --> 00:00:33.689
director of his era.

00:00:33.689 --> 00:00:36.231
He's David Lynch, and
here's my take on his Top 10

00:00:36.231 --> 00:00:37.436
Rules of Success.

00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:39.328
Rule number nine is my personal favorite,

00:00:39.328 --> 00:00:40.405
and I'd love to know which one

00:00:40.405 --> 00:00:41.938
you guys like the best.

00:00:41.938 --> 00:00:43.981
And as always, guys, as
you're watching the videos,

00:00:43.981 --> 00:00:46.146
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:47.953
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:47.953 --> 00:00:49.365
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:49.365 --> 00:00:50.787
can be inspired as well.

00:00:50.787 --> 00:00:52.912
Also, as you are writing something down,

00:00:52.912 --> 00:00:56.172
it's much more likely to
stick in your head too.

00:00:56.172 --> 00:00:57.005
Enjoy.

00:00:59.951 --> 00:01:02.701
(dramatic music)

00:01:09.957 --> 00:01:13.920
- [David] An idea comes, and
you see it, and you hear it,

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.737
and you know it.

00:01:15.737 --> 00:01:17.119
- How does it come?

00:01:17.119 --> 00:01:20.884
- It comes like on a TV in your mind.

00:01:20.884 --> 00:01:23.884
(audience laughing)

00:01:29.417 --> 00:01:32.314
- You know, there's a
line I've always loved

00:01:32.314 --> 00:01:33.653
of Leonard Cohen,

00:01:33.653 --> 00:01:37.361
he said, if I knew where
the good songs came from,

00:01:37.361 --> 00:01:39.332
I would go there more often.

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:41.117
(audience laughing)
- Absolutely.

00:01:41.117 --> 00:01:44.974
People, we want, we don't
do anything without an idea.

00:01:44.974 --> 00:01:47.352
So, they're beautiful gifts.

00:01:47.352 --> 00:01:49.851
And I always say, you,

00:01:49.851 --> 00:01:53.447
desiring an idea is like a bait on a hook.

00:01:53.447 --> 00:01:55.677
- Yeah.
- We can pull them in.

00:01:55.677 --> 00:01:58.664
And if you catch an idea that you love,

00:01:58.664 --> 00:02:01.576
that's a beautiful, beautiful day.

00:02:01.576 --> 00:02:05.618
And you write that idea
down so you won't forget it.

00:02:05.618 --> 00:02:09.226
And that idea, that you caught,
might just be a fragment

00:02:09.226 --> 00:02:12.672
of the whole, whatever
it is you're working on,

00:02:12.672 --> 00:02:14.988
but now you have even more bait.

00:02:14.988 --> 00:02:19.255
Thinking about that small
fragment, that little fish,

00:02:19.255 --> 00:02:20.838
will bring in more.

00:02:21.928 --> 00:02:24.741
And they'll come in and they'll hook on.

00:02:24.741 --> 00:02:26.378
And more and more come in,

00:02:26.378 --> 00:02:29.454
and, pretty soon, you might have a script.

00:02:29.454 --> 00:02:33.621
Or a chair, or a painting,
or an idea for a painting.

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:36.070
- But they come as,

00:02:37.078 --> 00:02:38.821
in small...

00:02:38.821 --> 00:02:42.388
- More often than not, small fragments.

00:02:42.388 --> 00:02:45.293
I like to think of it
as, in the other room,

00:02:45.293 --> 00:02:48.207
the puzzle is all together.

00:02:48.207 --> 00:02:52.249
But, they keep flipping in
just one piece at a time.

00:02:52.249 --> 00:02:53.763
- In the other room...

00:02:53.763 --> 00:02:54.906
- Over there.

00:02:54.906 --> 00:02:57.906
(audience laughing)

00:02:59.097 --> 00:03:01.698
- In a sense, David,
there's always another room,

00:03:01.698 --> 00:03:02.673
somewhere.

00:03:02.673 --> 00:03:05.628
- That's a beautiful thing to think about.

00:03:05.628 --> 00:03:07.428
- Let's think about it a bit.

00:03:07.428 --> 00:03:08.554
- No, you think about it.

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:11.012
(audience laughing)

00:03:11.012 --> 00:03:14.578
And my advice, is to do
what you truly believe in.

00:03:14.578 --> 00:03:17.179
Don't do something to impress any studio

00:03:17.179 --> 00:03:19.190
or some money people.

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:22.470
It always seems to backfire,
as far as I've seen.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:25.509
Do what you really, truly believe in.

00:03:25.509 --> 00:03:27.305
And learn by the doing.

00:03:27.305 --> 00:03:29.145
It's great to go to film school,

00:03:29.145 --> 00:03:32.039
and you can learn a lot
of intellectual knowledge,

00:03:32.039 --> 00:03:33.706
but, learn by doing.

00:03:34.741 --> 00:03:36.541
And, now, the cost has come down.

00:03:36.541 --> 00:03:38.174
You can really, really go.

00:03:38.174 --> 00:03:39.943
And then you can see how things go.

00:03:39.943 --> 00:03:42.421
There's lots of film
festivals you can enter into

00:03:42.421 --> 00:03:45.921
and see if you can catch some distribution

00:03:47.378 --> 00:03:49.856
or some financial help.

00:03:49.856 --> 00:03:53.506
If you have a golf ball
sized consciousness,

00:03:53.506 --> 00:03:54.957
when you read a book,

00:03:54.957 --> 00:03:58.268
you'll have a golf ball
sized understanding.

00:03:58.268 --> 00:04:01.865
When you look out, a golf
ball sized awareness,

00:04:01.865 --> 00:04:03.491
and when you wake up in the morning,

00:04:03.491 --> 00:04:06.158
and golf ball sized wakefulness.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:10.654
But if you could expand
that consciousness,

00:04:10.654 --> 00:04:13.607
then, you read the book,
more understanding.

00:04:13.607 --> 00:04:16.045
You look out, more awareness.

00:04:16.045 --> 00:04:19.730
And when you wake up, more wakefulness.

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:21.377
It's consciousness.

00:04:21.377 --> 00:04:23.543
And there's an ocean

00:04:23.543 --> 00:04:26.721
of pure, vibrant consciousness

00:04:26.721 --> 00:04:28.554
inside each one of us.

00:04:30.012 --> 00:04:33.677
And it's right at the
source and base of mind.

00:04:33.677 --> 00:04:35.710
Right at the source of thought.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:38.978
And it's also at the source of all matter.

00:04:38.978 --> 00:04:42.568
I loved two scripts by a
guy named Gregory Brooker.

00:04:42.568 --> 00:04:46.978
Spring of 61 and The
Fall and Rise of Glen.

00:04:46.978 --> 00:04:48.978
And those three I loved.

00:04:50.561 --> 00:04:52.044
- Yeah.

00:04:52.044 --> 00:04:54.101
And Mary wants you to direct it.

00:04:54.101 --> 00:04:55.280
She says, take a look at this

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:56.113
and see if you're interested

00:04:56.113 --> 00:04:59.287
in directing it.
- Right, right.

00:04:59.287 --> 00:05:01.658
- But everybody notes, first of all,

00:05:01.658 --> 00:05:05.038
this is not like anything
you've ever done before.

00:05:05.038 --> 00:05:05.871
- Right.

00:05:05.871 --> 00:05:06.704
I, um,

00:05:08.158 --> 00:05:09.621
it was curious.

00:05:09.621 --> 00:05:13.206
I'm reading the script,
and I'm feeling things.

00:05:13.206 --> 00:05:15.855
And, I've always said that,

00:05:15.855 --> 00:05:20.855
film is such a beautiful
language, it can do abstractions.

00:05:20.855 --> 00:05:23.109
It can do abstractions.

00:05:23.109 --> 00:05:26.420
And, abstractions, they're the combination

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:29.937
of all these different
elements you have in a film.

00:05:29.937 --> 00:05:32.802
It can do an abstraction of emotion.

00:05:32.802 --> 00:05:36.122
And, I felt this emotion
coming from the script.

00:05:36.122 --> 00:05:39.290
And that's what I really,
that's what lit my fire.

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:41.057
That thing.
- The emotion of what?

00:05:41.057 --> 00:05:45.256
- The emotion coming from a
scene, from a word, from a look.

00:05:45.256 --> 00:05:47.012
Um, then when you read something,

00:05:47.012 --> 00:05:49.644
we all have the experience,
we picture it in our minds.

00:05:49.644 --> 00:05:52.773
And, those pictures, those initial things,

00:05:52.773 --> 00:05:54.967
are what guide us from then on.

00:05:54.967 --> 00:05:57.637
And so, as this thing, you
know, I was picturing it,

00:05:57.637 --> 00:06:00.451
I, at the same time, I'm feeling things,

00:06:00.451 --> 00:06:03.364
and I'm thinking, film can do this

00:06:03.364 --> 00:06:04.639
and it could be beautiful.

00:06:04.639 --> 00:06:06.670
This thing about emotion,

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:09.764
you know, it's an intuitive thing.

00:06:09.764 --> 00:06:12.133
The film talks to you.

00:06:12.133 --> 00:06:14.066
And, the story talks to you.

00:06:14.066 --> 00:06:18.800
And, uh, so, it's a little
bit action and reaction.

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.633
You know, you're experimenting as you go

00:06:21.633 --> 00:06:23.981
to get the thing to feel correct.

00:06:23.981 --> 00:06:27.036
And, how come, it has
to feel correct to me.

00:06:27.036 --> 00:06:31.011
And, um, so you work and work
until it does feel correct.

00:06:31.011 --> 00:06:34.566
I think it's very important, sometimes,

00:06:34.566 --> 00:06:36.399
to look at older work.

00:06:37.267 --> 00:06:41.747
And it can give you ideas
for the present work.

00:06:41.747 --> 00:06:44.664
And, or, even ideas for the future.

00:06:45.831 --> 00:06:49.041
It's kind of important
to sometimes go back.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:52.832
And I felt that same way about
seeing films with my son.

00:06:52.832 --> 00:06:54.455
I was glad I went back,

00:06:54.455 --> 00:06:57.986
and it kind of inspired
some things for the future.

00:06:57.986 --> 00:07:02.267
And, uh, it was, basically,
a good experience.

00:07:02.267 --> 00:07:04.745
I, personally, I love restrictions,

00:07:04.745 --> 00:07:06.601
because it forces you to create,

00:07:06.601 --> 00:07:08.773
in this, kind of like, boundaries.

00:07:08.773 --> 00:07:10.723
And maybe you think up neat ideas,

00:07:10.723 --> 00:07:12.938
and, because of these restrictions.

00:07:12.938 --> 00:07:13.925
Always, in a film,

00:07:13.925 --> 00:07:17.955
you wish that you could
be doing something else.

00:07:17.955 --> 00:07:19.125
You want to escape.

00:07:19.125 --> 00:07:20.672
And I think it's like,

00:07:20.672 --> 00:07:22.307
this pressure and responsibility,

00:07:22.307 --> 00:07:25.772
it's just, yearning to be doing anything,

00:07:25.772 --> 00:07:26.991
you know, and going anywhere.

00:07:26.991 --> 00:07:29.020
So many times, going to
the studio in the morning,

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:30.082
I wish the driver would, you know,

00:07:30.082 --> 00:07:33.074
become crazy and take me anywhere.

00:07:33.074 --> 00:07:36.476
But, when you get there,
you're back in it, and working.

00:07:36.476 --> 00:07:40.419
In spite of the restrictions,
it's such a fantastic process.

00:07:40.419 --> 00:07:43.313
And it's creating, and
creating is the coolest thing

00:07:43.313 --> 00:07:45.220
that we can do.

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:46.053
For me,

00:07:47.132 --> 00:07:48.799
what has happened is

00:07:49.656 --> 00:07:50.489
the

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:53.320
heavy weight of

00:07:54.783 --> 00:07:56.450
anxieties and fears,

00:07:57.546 --> 00:08:01.337
those things have gotten
less and less and less.

00:08:01.337 --> 00:08:02.170
And,

00:08:03.074 --> 00:08:07.297
for me, life has gotten
to be more like a game.

00:08:07.297 --> 00:08:09.547
And the things that used to

00:08:11.068 --> 00:08:12.318
almost kill me,

00:08:13.799 --> 00:08:15.849
don't kill me so much.

00:08:15.849 --> 00:08:18.674
And the enjoyment of doing

00:08:18.674 --> 00:08:20.174
gets so beautiful.

00:08:21.498 --> 00:08:24.622
And the ideas are flowing more and more.

00:08:24.622 --> 00:08:27.152
And the thrill of it all.

00:08:27.152 --> 00:08:28.513
The thrill of it all.

00:08:28.513 --> 00:08:30.586
I appreciate people more.

00:08:30.586 --> 00:08:34.019
I see people, and I see their faces and I,

00:08:34.019 --> 00:08:37.044
I feel like they seem familiar.

00:08:37.044 --> 00:08:40.377
And, I just don't have so many problems.

00:08:41.516 --> 00:08:44.219
And life gets more and more beautiful.

00:08:44.219 --> 00:08:47.908
And I still love the
same things, you know,

00:08:47.908 --> 00:08:50.041
painting and making films.

00:08:50.041 --> 00:08:51.820
It just gets better and better.

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:53.730
That's my experience.

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:55.872
Be true to yourself.

00:08:55.872 --> 00:08:58.289
Have your own voice ring out.

00:08:59.534 --> 00:09:01.938
Other things can inspire you,

00:09:01.938 --> 00:09:04.021
but, find your own voice.

00:09:04.977 --> 00:09:06.735
Be true to that voice,

00:09:06.735 --> 00:09:09.368
don't let anybody fiddle with it.

00:09:09.368 --> 00:09:12.050
Never turn down a good idea,

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:14.488
but never take a bad idea.

00:09:14.488 --> 00:09:16.386
And be true to the ideas.

00:09:16.386 --> 00:09:19.628
All the way along, every element.

00:09:19.628 --> 00:09:22.290
When you finish a film,

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:24.707
your job, basically, is over.

00:09:26.098 --> 00:09:29.598
And, you have a desire to share your work,

00:09:30.861 --> 00:09:33.410
and so, you put it out into the world.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:37.844
And you realize, that, yes,
you can help the film some

00:09:37.844 --> 00:09:40.677
by giving interviews or you can go

00:09:42.232 --> 00:09:44.732
to some places and have talks.

00:09:45.845 --> 00:09:49.151
But, basically, the film is on it's own.

00:09:49.151 --> 00:09:51.121
And you can't tell whether
it's going to go good,

00:09:51.121 --> 00:09:52.881
or going to go bad.

00:09:52.881 --> 00:09:55.662
It's important that you
believe in your work.

00:09:55.662 --> 00:09:56.495
And that's

00:09:59.168 --> 00:10:00.964
the most important thing.

00:10:00.964 --> 00:10:02.933
Transcendental Meditation is, obviously,

00:10:02.933 --> 00:10:06.672
the most important thing
to do day in and day out.

00:10:06.672 --> 00:10:11.622
Transcending is the key to
a better and better life.

00:10:11.622 --> 00:10:15.564
And this idea is catching on
more and more in the world.

00:10:15.564 --> 00:10:17.929
The receptivity to
Transcendental Meditation

00:10:17.929 --> 00:10:20.632
is growing by leaps and bounds

00:10:20.632 --> 00:10:22.602
thanks to so many in this room

00:10:22.602 --> 00:10:25.284
and so many others around the world.

00:10:25.284 --> 00:10:27.052
It's really happening.

00:10:27.052 --> 00:10:29.380
It's built for the human being.

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:31.365
And transcending our nervous system,

00:10:31.365 --> 00:10:33.853
it's built to transcend and experience

00:10:33.853 --> 00:10:37.938
that unbounded, eternal,
infinite, immortal,

00:10:37.938 --> 00:10:41.982
immutable ocean of pure
consciousness within.

00:10:41.982 --> 00:10:44.024
It's money in the bank.

00:10:44.024 --> 00:10:45.111
(audience laughing)

00:10:45.111 --> 00:10:46.691
Meditate regularly.

00:10:46.691 --> 00:10:49.994
And then, the second thing,
I do, is drink coffee.

00:10:49.994 --> 00:10:52.116
(audience laughing and applauding)

00:10:52.116 --> 00:10:52.949
- [Man On Stage] Your coffee.

00:10:52.949 --> 00:10:55.116
- [David] Yeah, my coffee.

00:10:57.943 --> 00:10:58.820
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:01.615
I made this video
because Luke asked me to.

00:11:01.615 --> 00:11:02.652
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:11:02.652 --> 00:11:04.276
that you want me to profile next,

00:11:04.276 --> 00:11:05.638
leave it down in the comments below

00:11:05.638 --> 00:11:06.785
and I'll see what I can do.

00:11:06.785 --> 00:11:08.839
I'd also love to know
what did David say here

00:11:08.839 --> 00:11:10.302
that resonated the most with you?

00:11:10.302 --> 00:11:11.983
What are you going to take from this video

00:11:11.983 --> 00:11:14.895
and immediately apply it to
your life or business somehow?

00:11:14.895 --> 00:11:15.728
Leave it in the comments

00:11:15.728 --> 00:11:17.426
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:11:17.426 --> 00:11:19.559
Finally, I want to give a
quick shout-out to Luke.

00:11:19.559 --> 00:11:21.760
Is that the same Luke
who requested the video?

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:22.879
Shout-out to Luke.

00:11:22.879 --> 00:11:25.399
Thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:11:25.399 --> 00:11:26.313
Your One Word,

00:11:26.313 --> 00:11:29.076
and making that awesome
art piece around Hustle,

00:11:29.076 --> 00:11:31.160
which, I guess, is your one word as well.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:32.815
I really appreciate the support.

00:11:32.815 --> 00:11:34.086
So, thank you, guys, again, for watching.

00:11:34.086 --> 00:11:35.081
I believe in you.

00:11:35.081 --> 00:11:37.113
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:11:37.113 --> 00:11:39.357
and whatever your one word is.

00:11:39.357 --> 00:11:41.774
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:11:49.582 --> 00:11:51.005
(train roaring by)

00:11:51.005 --> 00:11:51.838
- I spit up.

00:11:51.838 --> 00:11:52.903
- [Stagehand] Yeah?

00:11:52.903 --> 00:11:55.486
- But the spit up keeps coming.

00:11:58.488 --> 00:12:00.035
Jeremy, what are you doing?

00:12:00.035 --> 00:12:00.868
- [Jeremy] Sorry.

00:12:00.868 --> 00:12:01.701
- What are you doing?

00:12:01.701 --> 00:12:03.499
- [Jeremy] Just answering
a phone call, I'm sorry.

00:12:03.499 --> 00:12:04.536
- Okay, now come over here.

00:12:04.536 --> 00:12:05.597
- All right.
- You're in the shot.

00:12:05.597 --> 00:12:08.121
Take, you know, you've
seen this kind of crazy,

00:12:08.121 --> 00:12:10.347
you know, many times tonight.

00:12:10.347 --> 00:12:12.033
Greg.
- Greg.

00:12:12.033 --> 00:12:12.866
- And you just hold it

00:12:12.866 --> 00:12:13.699
like this.
- Okay.

00:12:13.699 --> 00:12:14.800
- And you guys just get up there and

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:16.839
you stand right next to her.

00:12:16.839 --> 00:12:18.522
Just like this and,

00:12:18.522 --> 00:12:19.418
I got it!
- I got it.

00:12:19.418 --> 00:12:21.865
- Yeah, and then start
bouncing up and down.

00:12:21.865 --> 00:12:23.766
Yeah, yeah, yeah, okay.

00:12:23.766 --> 00:12:25.490
Bounce around and kissing.

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:27.086
Yeah, okay, yeah.

00:12:27.086 --> 00:12:28.161
Okay.

00:12:28.161 --> 00:12:32.506
You got 30 seconds, this
is absolutely unforgivable.

00:12:32.506 --> 00:12:33.572
- [Actress] It has
nothing to do with them.

00:12:33.572 --> 00:12:34.842
- I don't care.

00:12:34.842 --> 00:12:36.122
Unforgivable.

00:12:36.122 --> 00:12:38.261
We could have shot this scene three times.

00:12:38.261 --> 00:12:41.936
How many minutes will be
Justin and Laura ready?

00:12:41.936 --> 00:12:43.615
On the set, in the bed.

00:12:43.615 --> 00:12:45.850
She leans against this column,

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:47.525
I'm on her.

00:12:47.525 --> 00:12:49.799
And that's when you, (whistles)

00:12:49.799 --> 00:12:50.716
get in here

00:12:52.053 --> 00:12:52.887
with the monkey.

00:12:52.887 --> 00:12:53.720
Alfredo,

00:12:55.083 --> 00:12:57.044
don't paint the door.

00:12:57.044 --> 00:12:57.877
- [Alfredo] No?

00:12:57.877 --> 00:12:58.710
- Okay.

00:13:00.358 --> 00:13:01.775
This graffiti is.

00:13:04.905 --> 00:13:05.861
No, no, no.
- Like this.

00:13:05.861 --> 00:13:08.205
- You're going to shoot
yourself in the crotch.

00:13:08.205 --> 00:13:10.426
Mousumi is racing into
that chair with the monkey.

00:13:10.426 --> 00:13:11.442
- Uh-huh.

00:13:11.442 --> 00:13:13.275
- I come off, that chair is empty,

00:13:13.275 --> 00:13:14.108
- Right.

00:13:14.108 --> 00:13:15.954
- I come off and I go over here

00:13:15.954 --> 00:13:17.892
to Mousumi and the monkey.

00:13:17.892 --> 00:13:18.725
Okay?

00:13:18.725 --> 00:13:19.640
- Okay.

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:22.658
- Now, when I go over to
Mousumi and the monkey,

00:13:22.658 --> 00:13:23.806
you race into this chair.

00:13:23.806 --> 00:13:24.639
- Yes, exactly.

00:13:24.639 --> 00:13:25.472
(man talking)

00:13:25.472 --> 00:13:28.487
Yes, exactly, with
reflection of the light,

00:13:28.487 --> 00:13:31.271
oh, it was so beautiful.

00:13:31.271 --> 00:13:32.621
I remember it now.

00:13:32.621 --> 00:13:36.538
I need a very microscopic,
small amount, thick.

00:13:40.382 --> 00:13:41.373
Eric?
- Yeah?

00:13:41.373 --> 00:13:44.937
- I need a watch, a
cigarette, pack of matches,

00:13:44.937 --> 00:13:45.856
and the silk.

00:13:45.856 --> 00:13:47.442
Do we have a mirror ball?

00:13:47.442 --> 00:13:48.478
- [Stagehand] A mirror ball?

00:13:48.478 --> 00:13:49.589
- I'll tell you, take that Mylar,

00:13:49.589 --> 00:13:50.829
- [Stagehand] Uh-huh.

00:13:50.829 --> 00:13:52.162
- Wrap it around

00:13:53.734 --> 00:13:54.663
the rubber ball.

00:13:54.663 --> 00:13:56.246
- [Stagehand] Okay.

00:13:58.941 --> 00:14:01.349
- That's better than a disco ball, Augur.

00:14:01.349 --> 00:14:02.182
- And then, behind--

00:14:02.182 --> 00:14:03.408
- What do you mean, a staircase?

00:14:03.408 --> 00:14:05.907
- There's a staircase
right here that goes up.

00:14:05.907 --> 00:14:06.740
Which I didn't,

00:14:06.740 --> 00:14:07.783
- Didn't photo that.

00:14:07.783 --> 00:14:09.095
- Yeah, I didn't think of it.

00:14:09.095 --> 00:14:09.928
- Oh!

00:14:09.928 --> 00:14:13.345
I want to see that staircase so bad, man.

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:18.779
Eric?
- Yes?

00:14:18.779 --> 00:14:21.029
- Where's that hammer
you've been talking about?

00:14:21.029 --> 00:14:23.696
(drill buzzing)

00:14:28.522 --> 00:14:29.756
(stagehand calling out)

00:14:29.756 --> 00:14:31.056
- [David] Not yet!

00:14:31.056 --> 00:14:33.723
(drill buzzing)

00:14:38.789 --> 00:14:40.617
- [David] Sweet.

00:14:40.617 --> 00:14:41.712
There's Jay.

00:14:41.712 --> 00:14:43.989
(Jay talking)

00:14:43.989 --> 00:14:44.822
Good.

00:14:55.071 --> 00:14:57.491
- [Stagehand] So the only other

00:14:57.491 --> 00:14:58.324
moment.

00:14:58.324 --> 00:15:00.290
- We're still not shooting,
we're still not shooting.

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:01.331
Where's Julia?

00:15:01.331 --> 00:15:02.164
- [Stagehand] She's coming.

00:15:02.164 --> 00:15:03.139
- Where is Julia?
- She's coming.

00:15:03.139 --> 00:15:04.485
- Okay, she's not here.

00:15:04.485 --> 00:15:05.967
- [Stagehand] I'll tear
the pump off the cart.

00:15:05.967 --> 00:15:07.634
- Tear the pump off.

00:15:08.803 --> 00:15:09.636
- [Stagehand] What about Dominic,

00:15:09.636 --> 00:15:10.531
what do you want him, same thing?

00:15:10.531 --> 00:15:13.016
- Summer, night, why
would he be different?

00:15:13.016 --> 00:15:13.849
Mousumi?

00:15:14.847 --> 00:15:16.818
This is David.

00:15:16.818 --> 00:15:18.235
Hi, how ya doin'?

00:15:19.506 --> 00:15:20.339
Good.

00:15:20.339 --> 00:15:22.988
Are you ready to work Friday
night with the monkey?

00:15:22.988 --> 00:15:24.821
You're shaking.
- God.

00:15:24.821 --> 00:15:28.496
- Have you been taking drugs
while you've been here?

00:15:28.496 --> 00:15:29.452
(laughing)

00:15:29.452 --> 00:15:31.058
Okay, now listen, Willie,

00:15:31.058 --> 00:15:32.521
quit the drugs, okay?

00:15:32.521 --> 00:15:33.485
- Yeah. (laughing)

00:15:33.485 --> 00:15:35.191
- We'll start you in on
meditation really soon,

00:15:35.191 --> 00:15:36.024
okay, Willie?

00:15:36.024 --> 00:15:36.857
(laughing)

00:15:36.857 --> 00:15:38.667
Get the sticks in the meantime.

00:15:38.667 --> 00:15:39.500
Dreadful.

00:15:40.426 --> 00:15:41.259
Right here.

00:15:41.259 --> 00:15:42.445
- Do you need to get through?

00:15:42.445 --> 00:15:43.778
- Sorry.
- Sorry.

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:51.181
- Um.

00:15:51.181 --> 00:15:54.014
(woman screaming)

00:15:58.448 --> 00:16:00.364
- (laughing) Oh, man.

00:16:00.364 --> 00:16:02.012
Unbelievable.

00:16:02.012 --> 00:16:03.486
Oh, that is really good.

00:16:03.486 --> 00:16:04.584
- Dig down on that one.

00:16:04.584 --> 00:16:05.417
- Mm-mm.

00:16:05.417 --> 00:16:07.500
- And, you will use the--

00:16:08.353 --> 00:16:09.186
- Jeremy?

00:16:13.869 --> 00:16:14.797
Eric?

00:16:14.797 --> 00:16:18.130
(stagehands applauding)

